On 10 May 2026 at 15:39, Charles Plessy wrote:
| Also, if group authorship is not possible in Debian, we may need to
| wonder how to handle group authorship when it is done upstream.  In
| particular, in the R community there is a trend that when a package
| "foo" declares a MIT license, the only copyright information given
| is something like: "Copyright: the <foo> authors".

It's complicated :)

CRAN has gotten stricter here on new packages and wants this info too. So
this is also often coupled with a list of copyright holders in DESCRIPTION.
Grep'ing among my packages here I see for example 'vroom' which lists three
authors of included libraries in the Authors@R file in DESCRIPTION, and links
Copyright: to a file COPYRIGHTS in the inst/ directory (ensuring it is
installed).

  https://sources.debian.org/src/r-cran-vroom/1.7.1-1/DESCRIPTION
  https://sources.debian.org/src/r-cran-vroom/1.7.1-1/inst/COPYRIGHTS

Then sometimes there is also inst/AUTHORS (here from a package I wrote)

  https://sources.debian.org/src/r-cran-rcppspdlog/0.0.29-1/inst/AUTHORS

CRAN has had Authors@R, which it knows how to parse, for a decade and is now
putting the foot down and requiring it on all updates. This is probably the
closest we get to parsing something.

A quick bit of experimenting:

> pd <- packageDescription("vroom")   # there is also read.dcf 
> aar <- utils:::.read_authors_at_R_field(pd[["Authors@R"]])
> lapply(aar, \(p) if ("cph" %in% p$role) paste(p$given, p$family))
[[1]]
NULL

[[2]]
NULL

[[3]]
NULL

[[4]]
NULL

[[5]]
[1] "https://github.com/mandreyel/ "

[[6]]
[1] "Jukka Jylänki"

[[7]]
[1] "Mikkel Jørgensen"

[[8]]
[1] "Posit Software, PBC "

>
> do.call(cbind, sapply(aar, \(p) if ("cph" %in% p$role) paste(p$given, p$family)))
     [,1]                             [,2]            [,3]               [,4]                  
[1,] "https://github.com/mandreyel/ " "Jukka Jylänki" "Mikkel Jørgensen" "Posit Software, PBC "
>

So I extract Authors@R (and we can read DESCRIPTION files remotely too), and
use an internal R function to get Authors@R read, and broken up. When 'cph'
is seen, I extract given and family. Not perfect, but maybe a start?

Dirk

